"""Easy Read versions of presentation entries 0-76 (Easy Read / plain English)."""

EASY = {
    'presentation::Working memory difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Working memory is like a notepad in your mind. You hold things on it for a few seconds while you use them.\n• Some children have a smaller notepad. When there is a lot to hold, some of it falls off.\n• It is not a diagnosis. Nobody diagnoses it. You can help without one.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child loses track in the middle of a task.\n• They do only part of an instruction, or lose their place.\n• People may think they are not listening. Often the words just fell out.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give one instruction at a time. Or write the steps down and leave them where the child can see.\n• Use aids like checklists, word banks and number lines.\n• Ask "What will you do first?" instead of "Do you understand?"\n• Memory training games on a computer are not advised for school work.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The class teacher and the school.\n• An audiologist or a speech and language therapist, if hearing or understanding words may be part of it.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Audiologist: a person who checks hearing.',

    'presentation::Inhibition difficulty': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to stop or hold back. The child acts before they think.\n• Most of us have a quick brake in our mind. For this child, the brake comes late.\n• It is not a diagnosis. Nobody diagnoses it.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Calling out, butting in, or grabbing things.\n• Rushing work and missing the instruction.\n• The child knows the rule. It just comes to mind too late.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Reminders just before a tricky moment, like lining up or the yard.\n• Think time before hands go up. Or everyone writes an answer on a small whiteboard.\n• Praise the child quickly when they manage to wait.\n• Punishment afterwards does not change much on its own.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school, with a plan for the hard times of day.\n• CAMHS or primary care, if it happens everywhere and someone asks about a diagnosis.\n• If the child is in danger, the school follows its safety steps the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people's mental health.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Cognitive flexibility difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to switch. It may be hard to change task, rule or plan.\n• When a plan changes, the child must let go of the old plan. That takes them longer.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not being stubborn.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child sticks with one way of doing a sum, even when it is not working.\n• They get upset when the timetable changes, or a new teacher comes in.\n• It can be hard for them to see another person\'s side.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Warn the child about changes ahead of time. A picture timetable can show changes.\n• Teach words like "Plan A and Plan B".\n• Adults can say out loud: "This is not working, so I will try another way."\n• Give two or three choices, not a wide open task.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school, with plans for moves between classes and schools.\n• CDNT or CAMHS, if there is a lot of upset, rituals, or other questions.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CDNT: Children\'s Disability Network Team. A health team for children with disabilities.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people\'s mental health.\n• Rituals: things a person feels they must do in the same way each time.',

    'presentation::Planning and organisation difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to work out the steps to a goal. It is also hard to put them in order and manage time.\n• The child can do each step. Working out the steps is the hard part.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not laziness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Stories with no clear shape.\n• Projects left to the last night.\n• The child wants to start but cannot see where to begin.\nWHAT HELPS\n• At first, adults give the child the plan. Then they hand it over bit by bit.\n• Break long tasks into small parts with dates. An adult checks each part.\n• Teach a simple routine: goal, plan, do, check how it went.\n• Use timers and wall planners so time can be seen.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A family doctor, if the problem started after an illness or a head injury.\n• Telling a child to "try harder" does not help. Planning can be taught.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Routine: steps you do the same way each time.',

    'presentation::Task initiation difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to get started on a task. The child has the skill to do it.\n• Starting is its own skill. Once started, many children finish well.\n• It is not a diagnosis. Nobody diagnoses it.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Sharpening pencils, looking around, or asking to go to the toilet.\n• A blank page when others have begun.\n• Different things can cause this. The child may be worried about getting it wrong. Or they may find the task hard to read.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Make the first step small and clear. For example, "Write the date and the title."\n• Start with the child, then step away after the first line.\n• Make starting feel safe. Try "Just get three ideas down."\n• Saying "Get started" again and again rarely helps.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school, maybe with a daily check-in adult.\n• If the child also seems sad or has lost interest in things, adults should ask about this. The school follows its safety steps if needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Check-in adult: a named adult who meets the child for a short chat.',

    'presentation::Self-monitoring difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to check your own work or behaviour as you go.\n• The child can find and fix a mistake when asked. They just did not look.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not that the child does not care.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Work handed in without checking.\n• Not noticing that a friend is annoyed.\n• The child says a test went well when it did not. They are not lying. They did not notice.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A short checklist for the child\'s own common mistakes. They tick it before handing in.\n• A pause routine for the whole class: "Finished? Read it and point to each word."\n• Teach social signs, like body language, through stories or role-play.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If the child may be at risk of harm, talk to the school\'s safeguarding person. Where there is real concern, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Motivation and engagement with work': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how much a pupil puts into school work. It includes doing it, caring about it and thinking hard.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not fixed. It can change.\n• When a child stops trying, it is usually because trying stopped working for them.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Avoiding tasks, rushing to be finished, or clowning.\n• The child may try hard in some subjects but not others. That tells us something.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give work the pupil can succeed at most of the time. Show their progress.\n• Give real choices in the task.\n• A named adult who checks in often. This is often the biggest help.\n• Praise effort and how they worked, not "being smart". Rewards alone are not enough.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school. Check reading and maths, as a hidden difficulty is a common cause.\n• If a pupil talks about death or self-harm, act the same day. Do not leave them alone. Tell the school\'s safeguarding person and parents. Where needed, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Work rate and pace of output': 'WHAT IT IS\n• The pupil gets less work done than others, or takes much longer.\n• They often understand the work. Getting it onto the page is slow.\n• Many things can cause this, like slow handwriting, reading, spelling or worry. It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Unfinished copies and tests.\n• Copying from the board not done.\n• Homework that takes hours.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give fewer questions but keep them hard. For example, "Do the five hardest."\n• Give printed notes, so time goes on thinking, not copying.\n• Teach typing if handwriting is the problem. Check typing really is faster.\n• Set homework by time. Stop at the agreed time and write a note to the teacher.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• For State exams, the school applies to the SEC. Check the SEC\'s current rules first.\n• A family doctor, if the slowness is new, or comes with tiredness, headaches or clumsiness.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Homework completion and follow-through': "WHAT IT IS\n• Homework has five steps. Write it down, bring it home, start it, finish it, hand it in.\n• A pupil can get stuck at any one of these steps. Each step needs a different fix.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It does not always show how hard a child tries. Home life matters a lot.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The homework journal is not filled in.\n• Fights about homework at home.\n• Homework takes far longer than it should.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Find the step that is breaking and fix that one.\n• Agree a time limit between school and home. When time is up, stop and write a note.\n• A homework club, if homework at home is not realistic.\n• Punishments often miss the real problem.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If a child cannot do homework because they care for others, have no food, or may be harmed, follow child protection steps. Tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Organisation of materials, books and equipment': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to keep track of things. Books, copies, pencils and sports gear get lost or forgotten.\n• It is different from planning. A pupil can plan an essay well and still forget the copy it is in.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not that the child does not care.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Wrong books, or no pencil.\n• A messy bag, tray or locker.\n• Lost letters home.\n• Most pupils feel embarrassed by it and fed up being in trouble.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Use one colour for each subject. The copy, book and folder all match.\n• Pack the bag at the same time each evening, with a checklist. An adult checks at first.\n• Keep a spare pencil case in school.\n• A weekly bag clear-out with an adult. Then slowly stop.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and a key adult in school.\n• If things keep going missing or get damaged, think about bullying.\n• If a family cannot afford things, link them gently with school supports.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Key adult: one named adult in school who helps the child.',

    'presentation::Sustained attention in whole-class vs one-to-one': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Some pupils pay attention well with one adult. But they drift off in a busy class.\n• With one adult, there is less noise, quick help and one voice. In class, all of that goes.\n• It is not a diagnosis. The gap between the two is the clue.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child is fine at a small table with one adult.\n• They are lost when the teacher talks to the whole class for a long time.\n• Doing well in a test room does not mean nothing is wrong. Both can be true.\n• The cause may not be attention. It could be hearing, understanding words, or worry.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Seat the pupil near the teacher. Check in after giving instructions.\n• Short bits of teacher talk, then a task. Use pictures as well as words.\n• Less noise and clutter near their seat.\n• A private signal to help them tune back in.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher, with support in the classroom.\n• A family doctor, if the child has staring spells and does not respond.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Note-taking and recording from the board': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to copy from the board, or write notes while a teacher talks.\n• Copying asks for many things at once. Look up, find your place, remember words, look down, spell, write.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not laziness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Mistakes and missed lines when copying.\n• Running out of time.\n• Notes that come home not finished.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give printed notes, so the pupil adds to them and does not copy.\n• Give homework on a slip or online.\n• Seat the pupil with a clear, close view of the board.\n• In secondary school, teach note-taking step by step.\n• A laptop is not always the answer. Choose what is faster and more accurate for this pupil.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• An eye check, if the child squints, gets headaches or holds work close.\n• A family doctor, if copying or handwriting suddenly gets worse.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Study skills and revision strategy': "WHAT IT IS\n• The pupil works hard but studies in ways that do not stick well.\n• Re-reading and highlighting feel useful, but work less well.\n• Testing yourself works better, even though it feels harder.\n• It is not a diagnosis. Good study ways are often not taught.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Lots of hours spent, but results do not match.\n• Copying notes out again and again.\n• Studying late the night before.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Test yourself. Use flashcards, quizzes and past exam questions.\n• Short, regular sessions over weeks beat long ones the night before.\n• Turn each heading in your notes into a question. Answer it from memory.\n• A simple weekly plan, with an adult checking at first.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• Teachers can teach study skills to the whole class.\n• If there is panic, poor sleep or loss of hope, think about worry or low mood. If self-harm is mentioned, follow the school's safety steps.\n• If a pupil studies a lot but remembers little, check reading and language.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Self-harm: when a person hurts themselves on purpose.",

    'presentation::Help-seeking': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is about knowing when you need help, who to ask, and how to ask.\n• Pupils who need help most are often least likely to ask.\n• It is not a diagnosis. A pupil who never asks may be sitting lost. A pupil who always asks may rely on others too much.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Sitting quietly when stuck.\n• Or a hand always up.\n• Some children do not notice they are stuck. Some do not have the words to ask.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A quiet way to ask, like a help card or coloured cups on the desk.\n• Teach what to say: "I tried this. I am stuck here."\n• Praise asking: "Good question. That is just the time to ask."\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If a child will not ask any adult for help when hurt or scared, they may be unsafe. Talk to the school\'s safeguarding person. If there is real concern, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Fatigue, sleep and time-of-day effects on attention': "WHAT IT IS\n• Some pupils focus well at some times and badly at others. This may follow tiredness, sleep or hunger.\n• Tiredness can look just like an attention problem. So check sleep first.\n• It is not a diagnosis. Sleep problems are a question for a doctor.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Good mornings and poor afternoons, or the other way round.\n• Harder Mondays after a busy weekend.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Plan the hardest work for the pupil's best time of day.\n• Movement breaks, a snack and water at low times.\n• Share simple sleep tips with families. For example, regular bedtimes and screens out of the bedroom.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, if the child snores loudly, stops breathing in sleep, or is very sleepy by day.\n• Questions about medicine go to the doctor who gave it.\n• If a child is always worn out because of harm or neglect at home, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.\n• Neglect: when a child's basic needs are not met.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::AAC and communication access': 'WHAT IT IS\n• AAC means other ways to communicate, as well as or instead of speech.\n• It can be signs, like Lámh. It can be pictures, a communication book, or a talking device.\n• It is not a diagnosis. The aim is for the child to talk with anyone, anywhere.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Whether the child\'s system is there all day, in class, yard and at home.\n• Whether adults use it too.\n• Using AAC does not stop a child learning to talk. Research says it can help speech.\nWHAT HELPS\n• All adults point to the pictures as they speak. This is how the child learns it.\n• Give words for more than asking. Include words to say no, comment, joke and complain.\n• Give words to tell about harm, like "stop" and "hurt".\n• Do not make a child wait for speech before giving AAC.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• A speech and language therapist.\n• The school and the SENO, for equipment.\n• Children without speech are at more risk of abuse. If you are worried, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• AAC: Augmentative and Alternative Communication. Other ways to communicate besides speech.\n• Lámh: a sign system used in Ireland.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser. They arrange extra school supports.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::English as an Additional Language (EAL)': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is a pupil whose first language is not English. They are learning English, and often Irish, in school.\n• It is not a disorder and not a special need in itself.\n• Chatting with friends comes fast. The language of school books takes years longer.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Good playground English, but trouble with textbooks.\n• A new pupil may be quiet for a while at first. This is normal.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Pictures, key words taught before a topic, and a buddy.\n• Keep speaking the home language at home. It helps English, and does not harm it.\n• Watch progress over time. Compare with other pupils learning English.\n• Tests made for English speakers do not give fair scores here.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school's language support teachers.\n• A speech and language therapist, if there are problems in the home language too.\n• If there is any child protection worry, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• EAL: English as an Additional Language. English is not the pupil's first language.\n• Disorder: a real difficulty in how something develops.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Bilingual language development': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is a child learning two or more languages. This includes children in Irish-language schools.\n• It is not a disorder. Mixing languages when talking is normal.\n• Each language may look a bit behind on its own. Together, the child's words add up to about the same as other children's.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A child knows some words in one language and other words in the other.\n• A child in an Irish-language school may be a bit behind in English reading for a while. This is expected and usually passes.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Parents speak the language they are most comfortable in.\n• Count words in both languages when checking progress.\n• Link reading skills across the two languages.\n• Learning two languages does not cause language problems. Children with extra needs can learn two languages too.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A speech and language therapist, if there are problems in both languages, or parents are worried.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Bilingual: using two languages.\n• Disorder: a real difficulty in how something develops.",

    'presentation::Vocabulary gap from limited exposure': "WHAT IT IS\n• The child knows fewer words than expected for their age.\n• This is because they have had fewer chances to hear and use words. It is not a problem with learning words.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not a judgement on families.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child can read the words, but does not understand the text well.\n• When words are taught, the child learns them quickly. That is a good sign.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Teach useful words directly. Use them many times, in real sentences.\n• Teach key words before a new topic.\n• Talk back and forth with the child. The back and forth is what matters.\n• Shared reading in school.\n• Word lists alone are not enough.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A speech and language therapist, if the child learns new words slowly even with good teaching.\n• If there are signs of neglect, talk to the school's safeguarding person. Where needed, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Vocabulary: the words a person knows.\n• Neglect: when a child's basic needs are not met.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Participation in oral work and classroom talk': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how much a pupil talks in class. It covers answering, group work and talks.\n• Talking in class helps pupils learn and think.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not just shyness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The pupil rarely puts up their hand.\n• One-word answers.\n• Avoiding reading aloud.\n• The cause may be language, worry, a stammer, or something else.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Wait a few seconds after a question before taking answers.\n• Let pupils talk in pairs first, then share with the class.\n• Build up slowly. Start with thumbs or whiteboards, then pairs, then groups.\n• Do not push a pupil to talk. Pressure makes it harder.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A speech and language therapist or CAMHS, if a child talks at home but not at all in school.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people's mental health.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Following multi-step verbal instructions': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when a child loses part of a spoken instruction with more than one step.\n• It can happen for different reasons. The child may not hear it well, or not understand the words. Or it may be hard to hold in mind.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not being defiant or not caring.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child does only the first or last part.\n• They stop, copy others, or ask again.\n• It may look fine at home. Home instructions are usually shorter and more familiar.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give steps one at a time, in the order they are done.\n• Write the steps on the board, or use pictures.\n• Teach the child to ask: "Can you say the second bit again?"\n• Saying "listen better" is not a plan.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, for a hearing check.\n• A speech and language therapist, if understanding words is the problem.\n• CDNT, if needs are complex.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CDNT: Children\'s Disability Network Team. A health team for children with disabilities.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Narrative and sequencing in spoken account': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how a child tells a story or says what happened.\n• Does it have a start, middle and end? Can the listener follow who did what?\n• Telling a story in order is one of the hardest things we do with language.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Events come out of order.\n• The story may change when told again. This does not mean the child is lying.\n• It can be hard for the child to tell a teacher what happened in the yard.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A picture story frame: who, where, what happened, how they felt, how it ended.\n• Teach words like first, next, then, because and in the end.\n• After an incident, let the child draw it in boxes, like a comic strip.\n• At home, ask about the day in steps. Use photos to help.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A speech and language therapist, or CDNT if needs are complex.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CDNT: Children's Disability Network Team. A health team for children with disabilities.\n• Incident: something that happened, often a fight or upset.",

    'presentation::Word-finding difficulty': 'WHAT IT IS\n• The child knows a word, but it will not come out when they need it.\n• It is like a name on the tip of your tongue, but it happens more often.\n• It is not the same as not knowing the answer. It is not a stammer, though both can happen together.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Long pauses, or "um" and "the thing".\n• Describing the word instead, like "the thing you cut with" for scissors.\n• The child may stop putting up their hand.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give the child time. Do not finish their sentences.\n• Tell them the question before you ask it in class.\n• Let them point, choose or write instead.\n• Teach tricks: say what it is used for, or think of the first sound.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• A speech and language therapist.\n• For State exams, check the current rules. Do not promise extra help.\n• If a child suddenly loses words they used before, see a family doctor quickly.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Stammer: when speech gets stuck, or sounds repeat.',

    'presentation::Interpreter need and how it changes the assessment': "WHAT IT IS\n• Sometimes a child or parent does not share a language well with the psychologist.\n• Then a trained interpreter is needed. This is not a problem in the child.\n• It changes how the assessment is done.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Parents need to agree in a language they understand.\n• Parents' story about the child's home language is very useful.\n• Some tests only work properly in English. Their scores do not count when words are translated.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Use a trained interpreter. Tell them what to expect before the meeting.\n• Do not use family members to interpret. Never use children.\n• In the report, say an interpreter was used, and what that means for scores.\n• Give feedback in plain words. Check the parent understood.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• A trained interpreter, booked through NEPS.\n• A speech and language therapist, if there may be problems in both languages.\n• If a child protection worry comes up, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Interpreter: a person who changes speech from one language to another.\n• Assessment: finding out how a child learns and what helps.\n• NEPS: National Educational Psychological Service. School psychologists in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Cognitive profile differences (verbal, fluid, working memory, processing speed)': "WHAT IT IS\n• Some tests look at different thinking skills. A child may be much stronger in some skills than others.\n• This is a pattern in scores. It is not a condition and not a diagnosis.\n• Uneven patterns are common. Many children have them.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A child who understands everything but finishes nothing.\n• They may know more than their copybook shows.\n• A pattern of scores cannot tell you if a child has dyslexia or ADHD.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Plan for what the child needs in class, not for a label.\n• If the child works slowly, cut copying and give printed notes.\n• If holding things in mind is hard, give short steps and checklists.\n• Use the child's strengths as a way in.\n• Brain training games are not advised. They do not reliably help school work.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with a support plan.\n• CAMHS, CDNT or a speech and language therapist, if someone is asking about a diagnosis.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder. It affects attention, activity and self-control.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• CDNT: Children's Disability Network Team.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Underachievement not meeting SLD criteria': "WHAT IT IS\n• The child is doing less well in school than we would expect.\n• But it does not fit the rules for a specific learning disorder, like dyslexia.\n• That does not mean there is no problem. It is real, and it needs help.\n• Often a few things add up, rather than one cause.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child falls behind classmates, often in the older classes.\n• People may call the child lazy. That is not fair. Look for what is underneath.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A child does not need a label to get help.\n• Give extra teaching in the weak area. Check progress by a set date.\n• How the child responds tells us a lot.\n• Also look at attendance, worry, sleep and friends.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, through its support plan.\n• Tusla's school attendance service, if attendance is a problem.\n• If a child's work drops sharply, with sadness or missed days, think about bullying, mental health or home. Follow safety steps if needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SLD: Specific Learning Disorder, like dyslexia. It affects reading, writing or maths.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Giftedness / exceptional ability': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is a child who is far more able than their classmates in one or more areas.\n• There is no single agreed meaning.\n• Being very able does not mean the child will always do well. They still need the right teaching.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Boredom, or behaviour problems, when work is too easy.\n• Fear of getting things wrong.\n• A quiet child doing just enough.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give work that makes the child think harder, not just more of the same.\n• Let them skip what they already know.\n• Help them learn to cope with getting things wrong.\n• Skipping a year needs a careful plan and school agreement.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• CTYI runs programmes for able young people. Check costs first.\n• Being able does not protect against worry or self-harm. If you see these, follow the school's safety steps.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CTYI: Centre for Talented Youth Ireland, at Dublin City University.\n• Self-harm: when a person hurts themselves on purpose.",

    'presentation::Twice exceptional (high ability plus SLD, ADHD or ASD)': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is a child who is very able, and also has a learning difficulty or other condition.\n• Dyslexia, ADHD, autism and DCD are common examples.\n• The two can hide each other. Being clever can cover up the difficulty.\n• It is a way to describe a child, not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Work that looks average, so school sees no problem.\n• A child who is frustrated, very tired or worried at home.\n• Strong at reasoning with words, but weak at spelling or writing.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give both things at once: challenge where the child is strong, and support where they are not.\n• Let them type, talk or use a scribe, so writing does not hold them back.\n• Do not take away extra challenge to "fix the weak spots".\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with a plan that names both the strength and the need.\n• CAMHS, CDNT, a speech and language therapist or an occupational therapist, if a diagnosis may be needed.\n• If there is strong worry, school avoidance or low mood, follow the school\'s safety steps where needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SLD: Specific Learning Disorder, like dyslexia.\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder.\n• ASD: Autism Spectrum Disorder.\n• DCD: Developmental Coordination Disorder. It affects movement.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• CDNT: Children\'s Disability Network Team.',

    'presentation::Understanding and responding to adult direction': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how a child responds when an adult tells them what to do.\n• There are three main reasons a child may not do it. They did not understand. They could not get started. Or they did not want to.\n• The help is different for each. So first check if the child understood.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A child who follows familiar directions but not new or long ones.\n• A child who responds to one teacher but not another.\n• Words like "defiant" guess at why. It is better to say what happened.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Say the child\'s name first. Give short, clear directions, one at a time.\n• Add a picture or a gesture. Ask the child to say what they will do.\n• Give directions quietly and privately. Wait a little before repeating.\n• Check understanding before adding punishments.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, for a hearing check.\n• A speech and language therapist, for understanding of language.\n• If a child is frightened of adults or freezes, think about their safety. If there is concern, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Defiant: choosing not to do what you are asked.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Curriculum access at the current level': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is about whether a child can really learn from what their class is being taught.\n• It is not just being in the room. It is understanding, joining in and making progress.\n• It is about the fit between the child and the teaching.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• The child works well below the class level.\n• The gap may grow in the older classes.\n• A child close to their age level may still struggle, for example because of worry.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Change the work so the child can join in. Say which parts of the lesson they can join, and who helps.\n• A plan with goals at the child's level, checked each term.\n• In Ireland, children are included in mainstream classes first. Try and describe mainstream supports before other options.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and the special education teacher.\n• The SENO can explain other options, such as a special class.\n• Parents decide with the school and the SENO. The psychologist gives information to help.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Curriculum: what is taught in school.\n• Mainstream: an ordinary class or school.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser. They arrange extra school supports.",

    'presentation::Interrupted or missed schooling': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is a child who has missed a lot of school. It could be from illness, moving, or other reasons.\n• Some of what they find hard may be things they were never taught. That is fixable.\n• It is not a learning difficulty in itself.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Gaps in basic reading and maths.\n• A child who missed early phonics may look like they have a reading difficulty.\n• Test scores may not show the child's real ability.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Find the gaps and teach them directly.\n• See how the child responds to teaching before thinking about anything else.\n• Going to school regularly is the biggest help. If something makes that hard, talk about it.\n• A key adult and a planned return after a long absence.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and Tusla's school attendance service.\n• CAMHS or other services, where needed.\n• If absence is unexplained or growing, with signs of neglect or risk, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Phonics: linking letters to sounds to read.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Neglect: when a child's basic needs are not met.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Auditory processing concerns': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is a worry that a child finds it hard to make sense of what they hear.\n• Their hearing test is normal.\n• Only an audiologist can diagnose auditory processing disorder. Experts do not all agree about this condition.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "He hears but does not understand."\n• Trouble in a noisy classroom.\n• Mishearing words, or finding phonics hard.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Cut background noise. Seat the child near the teacher.\n• Face the child when you speak. Show key information in writing or pictures too.\n• Check they understood. Teach key words first.\n• Do not wait for a test before making classroom changes.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, for a full hearing test first. Do this before anything else.\n• A speech and language therapist, to check language.\n• An audiologist. This test may be hard to get here, so check what is available.\n• A sudden change in hearing needs a doctor urgently.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Audiologist: a person who checks hearing.\n• Phonics: linking letters to sounds to read.\n• Diagnose: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Literacy difficulty not meeting SLD criteria': "WHAT IT IS\n• The child's reading, writing or spelling is behind where we would expect.\n• But it does not fit the rules for dyslexia at the moment.\n• That does not mean there is no problem. The child still needs extra help.\n• Reading difficulty is on a scale. There is no sharp line.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Low scores on school reading tests.\n• Teachers are worried about reading.\n• Each part of reading can be different: sounding out words, reading smoothly, understanding and spelling.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Extra teaching that matches the child's needs. Check progress by a set date.\n• Books at the right level, and new words taught first.\n• If progress stays slow after good teaching, give more support and think again.\n• Coloured overlays and eye exercises are not advised. There is not good evidence for them.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, through its support plan.\n• Dyslexia Ireland, for general information.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SLD: Specific Learning Disorder, like dyslexia. It affects reading, writing or maths.\n• Dyslexia: a lasting difficulty with reading and spelling.\n• Literacy: reading and writing.",

    'presentation::Reading stamina and avoidance of reading aloud': "WHAT IT IS\n• Some children get tired quickly when reading. Reading takes them a lot of effort.\n• Some children avoid reading aloud in front of others. It makes them feel worried or embarrassed.\n• You do not need a diagnosis to help.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Stopping reading after a short time.\n• Asking for the toilet, or feeling sick, at reading time.\n• This is not laziness. Reading has felt hard or upsetting for them.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Never ask the child to read aloud to the class without warning.\n• Let them practise a passage first, or read to one adult.\n• Short bits of reading with breaks. Audiobooks for long texts.\n• Extra reading practice in school, with progress checked.\n• At home, keep reading short and fun.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, through its support plan.\n• An eye check, if there are headaches or rubbing eyes.\n• If there is strong upset, tummy aches or missed school, look at worry too. Follow the school's safety steps where needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Stamina: being able to keep going for longer.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Handwriting speed and legibility under time pressure': "WHAT IT IS\n• The child's handwriting is slow, hard to read, or both. This is worse when time is short.\n• When writing takes effort, there is less room for ideas. The child may have good ideas but write little.\n• It is not laziness or carelessness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Writing gets messier when the child is rushed.\n• Running out of time in tests and copying.\n• The cause may be movement, spelling, or planning sentences.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Less copying. Give printed notes. Mark work for ideas, not neatness.\n• Extra time, or fewer questions.\n• Teach typing, and allow typed work.\n• A handwriting programme if forming letters is the problem.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• An occupational therapist, for the movement side.\n• For State exams, the school can gather evidence for the SEC. Check the current rules. No outcome can be promised.\n• If writing causes pain or gets worse, see an occupational therapist or doctor.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Legibility: how easy writing is to read.\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills, like movement and writing.\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Spelling as a barrier to written output': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Spelling is hard work for this child. So they write less than they could.\n• They may avoid words they cannot spell and keep sentences short.\n• Their ideas are often much better than their writing shows.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Short, simple writing.\n• Spoken ideas that are much richer than written ones.\n• Good copying, but poor spelling on their own.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Let the child write ideas first. Fix spelling after.\n• Give word banks for topic words.\n• Mark ideas first. Correct only a few common words.\n• Teach spelling clearly, step by step, based on their mistakes.\n• Spell-check, word prediction or speaking into a device can help.\n• Weekly spelling tests alone are often not enough.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, through its support plan.\n• If reading is hard too, and does not improve with teaching, a fuller check may help.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Word prediction: a tool that guesses the word you are typing.\n• Word bank: a list of useful words to use in writing.',

    'presentation::Assistive technology not yet trialled': "WHAT IT IS\n• Assistive technology means tools that help with reading and writing.\n• Examples are tools that read text aloud, or type what you say.\n• This entry is about a child who has not tried these tools yet.\n• The tools do not replace learning to read and write. They are used alongside teaching.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A child who finds reading or writing hard, with no tools tried.\n• Tools given with no training often go unused.\n• Using these tools is not cheating.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A planned trial. Name the tool, who will train the child, how long, and how to check it helps.\n• Let the child use the tool in class every day.\n• Show teachers and parents how to set it up.\n• Many tools are free and already on phones and laptops.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school's special education teacher.\n• The SENO, if special equipment is needed. The school applies. Check who can get it.\n• Dyslexia Ireland, for information.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assistive technology: tools that help you do tasks that are hard for you.\n• SENO: Special Educational Needs Organiser. They arrange extra school supports.",

    'presentation::Instruction history': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This means what a child has actually been taught. It covers what, how, how often and for how long.\n• We must know this before we decide a child has a learning difficulty.\n• It is not a judgement on the teacher or school. It is a list of facts.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "He has had learning support" is not enough detail.\n• We need to know which programme, how often, for how long, and what progress was made.\n• This matters for maths too, not only reading.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Write a short teaching history in the report.\n• If teaching has been limited, give good, planned teaching first. Then see how the child responds.\n• If good teaching did not help much, that supports more assessment and more support.\n• Keep clear records of extra teaching and progress.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and teachers, by keeping good records.\n• The psychologist, who uses this to understand test scores.\n• If the teaching history is not known, do not decide a child has a learning disorder.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Assessment: finding out how a child learns and what helps.\n• Learning disorder: a lasting difficulty with reading, writing or maths.',

    'presentation::Numeracy difficulty not meeting SLD criteria': 'WHAT IT IS\n• The child\'s maths is behind where we would expect.\n• But it does not fit the rules for dyscalculia at the moment.\n• Maths difficulty has many causes. Two children with the same score may need very different teaching.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Trouble with number facts, place value or word problems.\n• Some children are worried about maths. Others struggle to read the question.\n• Thinking "I am not a maths person" can hold a child back. Maths skills can be taught.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Extra maths teaching that matches the child\'s mistakes. Check progress by a set date.\n• Start with real objects, then pictures, then numbers.\n• Use number lines, and write down the steps.\n• Read word problems aloud, so it is the maths being tested.\n• Maths builds on itself. Filling the gaps makes a big difference.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, through its support plan.\n• If basic number sense stays hard after good teaching, more assessment may help.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SLD: Specific Learning Disorder. It affects reading, writing or maths.\n• Dyscalculia: a lasting difficulty with understanding numbers.\n• Numeracy: using numbers and maths.',

    'presentation::Maths anxiety': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Maths anxiety is feeling tense, afraid or dreading maths.\n• Worry takes up room in the mind. So the child does worse than they really can.\n• Doing badly can cause worry, and worry can cause doing badly.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not the same as a maths learning difficulty.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Upset during timed tables tests or answering at the board.\n• Tummy aches on maths days.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Less time pressure. Less answering in front of the class.\n• Show that mistakes are part of learning. Praise effort, not speed.\n• Teach any missing maths skills as well.\n• Teach a calming routine before tests.\n• Adults can avoid saying "I was never good at maths".\n• More timed tests or "toughening up" do not help.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• Primary Care Psychology or CAMHS, if worry is wider or severe.\n• If there is panic or school avoidance, follow the school\'s safety steps where needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Anxiety: strong worry or fear.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people\'s mental health.\n• Primary Care Psychology: a local health service psychologist.',

    'presentation::Number sense and estimation': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Number sense is a feel for how big numbers are.\n• For example, you know 48 is nearly 50 without working it out.\n• Estimating means guessing about how much. It is a skill that can be taught.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It does not prove dyscalculia.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Counting everything one by one, which is slow and tiring.\n• Using fingers for longer than classmates.\n• Not noticing when an answer is way off.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Use real objects and pictures before written numbers. Bead strings and tens frames are examples.\n• Play board games with a number track. Parents can do this at home.\n• Guess first, then work it out. Praise good guesses.\n• Timed tables tests do not build number sense.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school, with a small-group maths programme.\n• If progress stays slow after good teaching, more assessment may help.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Estimate: a sensible guess about a number.\n• Dyscalculia: a lasting difficulty with understanding numbers.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Recall of number facts vs procedural method': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This looks at where a child gets stuck in a sum.\n• It may be remembering quick facts, like 7 times 8. It may be the steps of a method. Or it may be why the method works.\n• Each needs a different kind of help. It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Counting on fingers for simple facts.\n• Saying tables from the start to find one answer.\n• Mixing up steps in a written method.\n• This is not about not trying. Many children practise hard and still cannot recall facts.\nWHAT HELPS\n• If facts are hard: teach shortcuts, like doubles. Give a tables square when the task is about something else.\n• If steps are hard: a step-by-step card, and worked examples to follow.\n• If the why is hard: go back to objects and pictures.\n• A few minutes of fact practice each day. Avoid public timed tests.\n• Weak fact recall should not hold a child back from harder maths.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• For State exams, check the current rules before promising anything.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Method: the steps you follow to do a sum.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Word problems': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Some children can do a sum but not the same sum in a story.\n• The problem may be reading the words, or understanding the language. Or it may be picturing what is happening, or the maths itself.\n• It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "Good at sums, cannot do problems."\n• If the child does well when the problem is read aloud, reading is the barrier, not maths.\n• Maths words can mean something different from everyday words, like "difference".\nWHAT HELPS\n• If reading is the barrier, read problems aloud to the child.\n• Teach the child to draw or act out the story before choosing a sum.\n• Ask: "What is happening? What do we know? What are we finding?"\n• Tricks like "altogether means add" can mislead. Stop using them.\n• Teach maths words clearly.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• A speech and language therapist, if understanding language is the problem.\n• For State exams, the school can gather evidence for the SEC. Check the current rules.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Working memory load in multi-step calculation': 'WHAT IT IS\n• The pupil can do each step of a sum. But they lose track when they must hold several steps in mind.\n• Holding too much at once makes anyone do worse.\n• Worry about maths can make this worse. It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Forgetting the carried number.\n• Losing a part answer in long sums.\n• Mental maths that falls apart after one step.\n• This is not carelessness. The mistakes happen at the same points.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Write every step down. Praise this. It is not babyish.\n• Squared paper and a step card on the desk.\n• Worked examples next to practice sums.\n• Print the sums on the sheet, so there is no copying.\n• Memory training games are not advised. Make the load smaller instead.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If there is a sudden change, think about health, sleep or mood first.\n• A low memory score does not mean ADHD.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ADHD: Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder. It affects attention, activity and self-control.\n• Working memory: holding things in your mind for a short time while you use them.',

    'presentation::Fine motor difficulty (handwriting, pencil grip)': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is when small, careful hand movements are hard.\n• It affects pencil grip, forming letters and writing speed. Cutting and buttons can be hard too.\n• It is not a diagnosis on its own. It is not laziness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Slow, hard-to-read writing.\n• Pressing hard, or a sore hand.\n• Writing much less than the child knows.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Short, regular handwriting practice, with letters taught clearly.\n• Less writing when writing is not the point. Use printed sheets and spoken answers.\n• Learn to type early. It is a real skill, not a shortcut.\n• A good chair and table height.\n• A pencil grip alone is not enough.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• An occupational therapist. They can check if it is part of a wider movement difficulty.\n• CDNT, if needs are complex.\n• For State exams, start gathering evidence early. Check the SEC's current rules.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills, like movement and writing.\n• CDNT: Children's Disability Network Team.\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Gross motor difficulty (PE, balance, ball skills)': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when big body movements are hard. Examples are balance, running, catching and kicking.\n• It is not about effort. It is not just "not being sporty".\n• It is not a diagnosis on its own. Children do not always grow out of it.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Last to be picked for teams.\n• Avoiding yard games.\n• Bumping into others. Slow to change for PE.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Change PE so the child can join in. Do not leave them out.\n• Use softer balls and shorter distances. The teacher picks teams, not captains in public.\n• Practise the real skill the child wants to do.\n• Find one active thing they enjoy, like swimming. Confidence matters.\n• Extra time and a private space to change.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• An occupational therapist or a physiotherapist.\n• CDNT, if needs are complex.\n• A doctor quickly, if the child loses skills, falls more, or has pain.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• PE: Physical Education. Sport and movement lessons in school.\n• CDNT: Children\'s Disability Network Team.\n• Physiotherapist: a person who helps with body movement.',

    'presentation::Sensory modulation difficulties (over- or under-responsive)': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is when a child's reaction to things they see, hear or feel is too big or too small.\n• Some sounds, like a hand dryer, may feel like an alarm.\n• Some children do not notice things, like a cut. Others seek lots of movement or touch.\n• It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Upset in the lunch hall or at assemblies.\n• Refusing to wear parts of the uniform.\n• Chewing sleeves, or moving a lot in class.\n• Other things can look the same, like worry or ear pain. So keep checking.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Change the place first. Use a quieter seat, and warn before bells.\n• A planned quiet space the child can go to.\n• Build movement into the day.\n• Ear defenders at some times. Soft clothes.\n• Help the child name what upsets them and choose what to do.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• An occupational therapist, to look at it in more detail.\n• Parents, who often know the triggers best.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills and senses.\n• Trigger: something that sets off a reaction.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.",

    'presentation::Sensory discrimination difficulties': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when it is hard to tell things apart through the senses.\n• The child notices the sound, sight or touch. But they read it less clearly.\n• It can be hard to judge how hard you are pressing or pushing.\n• It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Pressing too hard and breaking pencils.\n• Hugs or play that are too rough. The child does not mean it.\n• Mixing up letters that look alike, for longer than others.\n• Trouble with buttons they cannot see.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Pair a weak sense with a stronger one. Add pictures to spoken words.\n• Teach how hard to press: light, medium, heavy.\n• Teach one of two confusing letters at a time. Keep a letter strip on the desk.\n• Help with rough play. Explain it is about force, not meaning to hurt.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• First, check hearing and eyesight.\n• An occupational therapist, for a full check.\n• Check injuries, as the child may not notice or tell.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills, senses and movement.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Motor-based sensory difficulties': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This covers two things. One is holding the body upright. The other is planning new movements.\n• Sitting up takes more effort for some children. So they get tired and slump.\n• Some children find it hard to think of, plan and order a new movement.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not a bad attitude.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Tired by mid-morning. Leaning on the desk.\n• Wrapping legs round the chair.\n• Slow to learn a new dance, PE routine or getting dressed.\n• A bright child may look messy and slow in practical tasks.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Check the chair and table first. Feet should be flat on the floor.\n• Mix sitting and active tasks. Allow standing work.\n• Teach new movements in small steps. Say the steps out loud. Practise more.\n• Picture steps for dressing and setting up.\n• Saying "sit up straight" does not help. It is a body stamina issue.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• An occupational therapist or a physiotherapist.\n• A family doctor, if there is pain, joints slipping out, or loss of skills.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills and movement.\n• Physiotherapist: a person who helps with body movement.\n• PE: Physical Education. Sport and movement lessons in school.',

    'presentation::Presentation and care of written work': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how written work looks. It covers layout, neatness, crossing out and torn pages.\n• Messy work has many possible causes. It could be handwriting, spelling, planning, or worry.\n• Messy work can get lower marks, even when the ideas are good.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It does not show how much a child cares.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Messy copies. Letters drifting off the line.\n• Torn pages from rubbing out again and again.\n• Some of the most careful pupils have the messiest pages. They keep erasing to make it perfect.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Mark what the child knows apart from how it looks.\n• Give margins, squared paper for maths, and a model page.\n• Teach one neat line through mistakes instead of rubbing out.\n• Allow typing for longer work.\n• Colour-coded copies and a weekly bag check.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• An occupational therapist, if movement is the main cause.\n• Parents can talk to their family doctor, or CAMHS, if a need for perfection spreads beyond school work.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people's mental health.\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills, like writing.",

    'presentation::Self-care and independence skills at school': 'WHAT IT IS\n• These are the skills to get through the school day without help.\n• They include dressing for PE, laces, toileting, opening lunch boxes and managing belongings.\n• A child may manage at home with lots of time, but not in a busy school.\n• Too much adult help can stop a child learning to do things alone.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Trouble with buttons, laces or changing for PE.\n• Needing help with lunch or the toilet.\n• Adults doing tasks for the child to save time.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Teach one skill at a time, in small steps.\n• Start at the end. The adult does most of the zip, and the child pulls it to the top. Then a bit more each week.\n• Picture steps where the task happens.\n• Plan how adults will slowly step back.\n• Easier clothes, like velcro shoes.\n• Use the same words and steps at home and school.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and the SNA, with a plan.\n• An occupational therapist.\n• A family doctor, if a child starts wetting or soiling again. Sometimes this can be a sign of distress or abuse. Follow child protection steps where needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SNA: Special Needs Assistant. A school staff member who helps with care needs.\n• PE: Physical Education.\n• Occupational therapist: a person who helps with everyday skills.',

    'presentation::Behaviour that challenges': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is behaviour that puts the child or others at risk. Or it seriously limits their learning.\n• The words mean the behaviour challenges us to respond well. It does not mean the child is "challenging".\n• It is not a diagnosis. Most children with it do not need one to get help.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Refusing, disrupting, or hitting out.\n• Running out of class.\n• The same behaviour can have different reasons. It may be to escape a task, to get attention, or because of noise.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Describe what happens, not the child\'s character.\n• Find out what the behaviour does for the child. Then teach a better way to get the same thing.\n• A plan to prevent it, teach new skills, respond calmly, and keep everyone safe.\n• Punishment alone does not teach a new way.\n• Shorter school days only under the official rules, with a plan to return.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with a behaviour support plan.\n• CAMHS or primary care, where needed.\n• If there is a child protection worry, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Behaviour function (ABC analysis, trigger-behaviour-consequence)': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is a way to understand behaviour by what it does for the child.\n• ABC stands for what happened before, the behaviour, and what happened after.\n• Every behaviour meets a need. It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Behaviour often helps a child escape a task, get attention, or get something they want.\n• Some behaviour feels good, or eases discomfort.\n• Slower things matter too, like poor sleep, hunger or a bad morning.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Change what happens before. Make tasks easier, or give a choice.\n• Teach a better way to meet the same need. For example, a break card.\n• Make sure the old behaviour no longer works, and the new way works quickly.\n• Keep records. Check again after a few weeks. If nothing changed, think again.\n• "Attention-seeking" really means "attention-needing".\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and the psychologist, looking for patterns, not blame.\n• All staff who work with the child should know the plan.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• ABC: Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence. What came before, what the child did, and what came after.\n• Function: what a behaviour does for the child.',

    'presentation::Emotion regulation in the classroom': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is how a child notices and manages big feelings in school.\n• Children learn this slowly. First adults help them calm down. Later they learn to do it themselves.\n• When feelings get very big, the thinking part of the brain goes offline for a while.\n• It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Outbursts at changes, losing games, or hard tasks.\n• Shutting down, crying or going quiet. These are easy to miss.\n• Tummy aches or refusing.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A trusted adult who stays calm and uses few words at the peak.\n• A calm space the child can go to early.\n• Teach feelings words and calming tricks when the child is calm, not during an outburst.\n• First show you understand. Then set limits. Then solve the problem together.\n• Plan for hard moments, like changes.\n• Ask parents what helps at home, and use the same way in school.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• CAMHS or primary care, where worry or mood are a concern.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people's mental health.\n• Regulation: managing your feelings and body.",

    'presentation::Response to correction and repair after an incident': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is about how a child reacts when an adult corrects them.\n• It is also about whether they can put things right afterwards.\n• Some children hear "you are bad" instead of "that was wrong". So they get defensive.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not simply defiance.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Arguing back, crying, or tearing up work.\n• Saying "I am stupid".\n• Not wanting to go back to class after an incident.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Correct quietly and briefly. Talk about the behaviour, not the person.\n• Give a fresh start after each incident.\n• When calm, ask: "What happened? Who was affected? How can we put it right?"\n• Saying sorry and putting things right may need to be taught.\n• At home, say "that was unkind", not "you are unkind".\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and a named adult in school.\n• CAMHS or primary care, if low self-worth or worry are strong.\n• If a child says things like "I hate myself", act the same day. If a child seems afraid of harsh treatment, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Transitions between activities and between classes': 'WHAT IT IS\n• A transition is a change from one thing to the next. Examples are yard to class, or one lesson to another.\n• Changes ask a lot at once. You must stop, switch, know what is next, and cope with crowds and noise.\n• It is not a diagnosis. It is not defiance.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Slow to settle after yard.\n• Trouble lining up.\n• Upset at sudden changes, like a new teacher.\n• Corridors are often busy, with less supervision.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Show what comes next with a picture timetable.\n• Give a countdown before stopping a fun activity. Use a timer the child can see.\n• Give the child a job, like carrying equipment.\n• Start lessons with the same short settling task.\n• In secondary school, leaving class a little early, and a named adult to check in with.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If a child leaves the building, make a clear safety plan with parents and the principal.\n• If a child avoids some places, ask about bullying.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Transition: moving from one activity or place to another.\n• Diagnosis: when a professional gives a condition a name.',

    'presentation::Behaviour system fit': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This asks if the school\'s behaviour system works for this pupil.\n• The system means rules, rewards, sanctions and charts.\n• The question is about the system, not the child. It is not a diagnosis.\n• It is not a criticism of the school. Most systems work for most pupils.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A pupil who is "on red" most days and has stopped trying.\n• Rewards the pupil never reaches.\n• Being sent out of a lesson they wanted to escape. This can reward the behaviour.\n• Other children start to see them as "the bad one".\nWHAT HELPS\n• Adapt the system, but keep the same expectations.\n• Set small, reachable targets. Use rewards that matter to this pupil.\n• Use a private card instead of a public chart.\n• Praise the right behaviour quickly and clearly.\n• Teach skills the system expects, like waiting and asking for help.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with an individual plan.\n• The principal, if many pupils are struggling the same way.\n• Shorter school days must follow the official rules. Tusla must be told.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Sanction: a punishment, like detention.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Escalation and de-escalation pattern': "WHAT IT IS\n• A child's upsets often follow the same pattern each time.\n• It goes from calm, to building up, to a peak, and back to calm.\n• What helps is different at each stage. It is not a diagnosis.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Early signs, like going quiet, pacing or pushing work away.\n• Upsets that seem sudden often had earlier signs, or a bad night before.\n• Lots of talking at the peak makes it worse.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A written plan for each stage, shared with every adult.\n• Early on, notice gently. Offer a choice or a break.\n• At the peak, use few words and give space. Keep everyone safe. Move other pupils, not the child.\n• Holding a child is a last resort, only to stop harm, and must follow the rules.\n• After, give quiet time. Talk it through only when fully calm.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with parents. Ask what parents see at home.\n• CAMHS and other services, where needed.\n• If a child harms themselves or talks about wanting to die, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Escalation: when upset builds up.\n• De-escalation: when upset calms down.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.",

    'presentation::Behaviour as communication of an unmet learning need': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Sometimes behaviour is a response to school work the child cannot do.\n• For example, messing to avoid reading aloud, or clowning to hide not understanding.\n• Always check for this. But it is not always the answer.\n• It does not excuse the behaviour. It shows where to help.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Behaviour is worst in some lessons, like English or maths.\n• Sharpening pencils, toilet trips, or disruption during reading or writing.\n• Language problems are often missed.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Give work the child can do with effort.\n• Read aloud to them. Use word banks and pictures.\n• Teach the missing skill, like reading or language.\n• Do not ask them to read aloud without warning.\n• Keep behaviour goals and learning goals side by side.\n• If we help with the learning, the behaviour often settles too.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school and teachers.\n• A speech and language therapist, for language.\n• A hearing check, if hearing is in doubt.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Unmet need: something a child needs that they are not getting yet.\n• Disruption: behaviour that stops a lesson going well.',

    'presentation::Harmful or sexualised behaviour in children': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is sexual behaviour by a child that is not expected for their age. It may be forced, secret, or cause harm.\n• Some sexual behaviour is normal for children\'s ages, like curiosity about bodies.\n• Age, force, secrecy and age gaps are what matter.\n• The child may have been harmed too. It is always a child protection matter.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Sexual behaviour with much younger children.\n• Force or secrecy.\n• Sexual words or acts not expected for the child\'s age.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Follow child protection steps first.\n• A safety plan for all the children involved.\n• Body safety lessons for the whole class, like Stay Safe.\n• Keep the child in school with the right supervision, where safe.\n• Most children who behave this way do not go on to harm others as adults. Do not call them names like "offender".\nWHO CAN HELP\n• Tell the school\'s safeguarding person. Also tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\n• Tusla and the Gardaí find out what happened. The psychologist does not question the child about it.\n• Specialist services and CAMHS, where needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.\n• Gardaí: the Irish police.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.',

    'presentation::Adolescent substance misuse / dual diagnosis': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is a young person's use of alcohol, vapes or drugs that is harming school, health or safety.\n• Dual diagnosis means a mental health difficulty and drug or alcohol use together. Each can make the other worse.\n• Often the drug does a job for the young person. It may help them sleep or switch off worry.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Missed afternoons and tiredness.\n• Grades dropping in every subject.\n• Use by a younger child is rare. It is always a welfare worry.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Keep the young person in school. School and a trusted adult protect them.\n• A key adult with short, regular check-ins.\n• Support the worry, sleep or low mood behind it.\n• Do not promise to keep it secret when there is risk.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, local youth drug and alcohol services, and CAMHS.\n• The school psychologist does not test or treat drug use.\n• If there is risk right now, act the same day. Tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Welfare: a child's safety and wellbeing.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Unstructured time': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Some pupils cope in class but struggle at yard, lunch, corridors or lining up.\n• In class, the teacher leads and the rules are clear. At break, the rules change all the time.\n• The difference between class and yard is the clue. It is about the setting, not a bad child.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Trouble in games with changing rules, like tag or football.\n• Problems when lining up, or coming back into class after break.\n• Bullying often happens at these times. So check for it.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Add structure to break. Use games led by an adult, or a quiet area to choose.\n• Remind the child of the rules before going out.\n• Adults on duty walk around, watch and chat.\n• Teach game rules and joining-in words at a quiet time. Then practise in the yard.\n• A calm routine for coming back into class.\n• Do not take away yard time as a punishment. The child needs the play.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school, with a break-time plan.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Unstructured time: times with no set activity, like break.\n• Supervision: adults watching and helping.',

    'presentation::Supervision ratio and yard layout': "WHAT IT IS\n• Sometimes the problem is how break time is set up, not one child.\n• This is about how many adults are on duty, and where.\n• It is also about hidden corners, crowded spots and the yard layout.\n• It is not a criticism of staff.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Many problems in one corner of the yard that adults cannot see.\n• Crowding at doors.\n• Several children referred from one school, all about lunchtime.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Split the yard into areas for ball games, quiet play and other games.\n• Adults on duty walk a route that covers hidden corners.\n• Stagger break times or lining up.\n• Children who still struggle need their own plan too.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The principal and staff. The psychologist gives advice. The school makes the decisions.\n• If there is injury, sexual behaviour or bullying in a hidden spot, tell the principal and safeguarding person. Where harm is serious enough, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Supervision ratio: how many adults there are for the number of children.\n• Safeguarding person: the school staff member in charge of child protection.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Group dynamics and peer influence': "WHAT IT IS\n• Some behaviour is best understood by looking at the group, not just one child.\n• A child may act differently with different friends. They may have a role, like the clown or the follower.\n• Friends matter most in the teenage years. Groups can lead to good or bad behaviour.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• A child who is polite alone, but does the dare in a group.\n• A group that gets worse together.\n• A child who is picked on by the group.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Work with the whole class on what to do when someone is picked on.\n• Do not put only pupils with behaviour problems together in one group. It can make things worse.\n• Give the child another place to belong, like a club or team.\n• Plan seating and groups.\n• Splitting a group up with no plan often does not work.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If a group is involved in harm, weapons, or sharing images, follow child protection steps. Tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own. Call the Gardaí if there is danger now.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Peer: someone your own age.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.\n• Gardaí: the Irish police.",

    'presentation::Risk-taking without a diagnosis attached': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is behaviour that could hurt the young person or others. There is no diagnosis to explain it.\n• Examples are climbing, running from school, dares and unsafe online contact.\n• Many teenagers take more risks. It is partly how the brain grows, and friends make it stronger.\n• Risk-taking often does a job, like excitement or status. Ask what it does.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Running out of class or school.\n• Climbing walls or roofs. Dares in the yard.\n• Some risky acts are really self-harm. Ask about this directly. Asking does not put the idea in their head.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A safety plan first, when the risk is right now.\n• Offer safe ways to get excitement and status, like sport or leadership roles.\n• Practise ways to get out of a risky situation without losing face.\n• A key adult who checks in, especially on high-risk days.\n• Warnings alone do not work well.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with parents.\n• CAMHS, if there is low mood or self-harm.\n• If there is self-harm or talk of suicide, act the same day. If there is sexual risk, grooming or abuse, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school's safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Grooming: when someone builds trust with a child in order to harm them.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.",

    'presentation::Low confidence and self-esteem': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Self-esteem is how much you value yourself. Confidence is how sure you feel you can do things.\n• Children judge themselves in different areas, like school work, sport and friends.\n• It is not a diagnosis. But it is worth taking seriously.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "I\'m in the bottom group."\n• Feeling good about friends or sport, but not about school work.\n• Low self-esteem is not the same as low mood.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Help the child really succeed, with effort. Show progress against their own best.\n• Praise what they did, like "you checked your answer". Not "you are so clever".\n• Give a job that uses a real strength.\n• Fix the cause. If reading is hard, help with reading. That is the self-esteem help.\n• Telling a child they are great will not fix it on its own.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• The family doctor or CAMHS, if there is low mood or self-harm.\n• If a child says they are worthless or better off gone, ask about suicide. Act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-esteem: how you feel about yourself.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services. A health service for young people\'s mental health.',

    'presentation::Learned helplessness': 'WHAT IT IS\n• The child has come to believe that trying makes no difference. So they stop trying.\n• This makes sense after many failures. But it may not be true any more.\n• It is learned, so it can be unlearned. It is not a diagnosis. It is not laziness.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "I can\'t do it" before trying.\n• Waiting for help, copying, or a blank page.\n• Tears when asked to start alone.\n• It is common after years of an unnoticed reading or maths difficulty.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Break tasks into steps the child can do.\n• Every time something works, point to what the child did. They need to see this again and again.\n• Say success comes from effort and good ways of working, not from being clever.\n• Adults step back slowly on purpose. Try "try it first, then ask".\n• Teach any missing reading or maths skills.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• The family doctor or CAMHS, if the child feels hopeless about everything, or seems low.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Learned helplessness: believing nothing you do will help.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.',

    'presentation::Academic self-concept vs global self-esteem': 'WHAT IT IS\n• Academic self-concept is how a child sees themselves as a learner.\n• Global self-esteem is how they feel about themselves overall.\n• These are linked but different. A child may feel good about themselves, but think they are bad at reading.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Feeling good about friends, sport and family.\n• Feeling "no good" at one subject.\n• Being in a very able class can make a child feel less able.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Say exactly which area is low. For example, "low view of self as a reader".\n• Teach skills in that subject and show progress.\n• Less public comparison. Use private feedback.\n• Protect what is going well. Do not stop sport or music for extra lessons.\n• A general self-esteem programme is not the answer if the problem is one subject.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• If a child starts to say "I\'m stupid, I\'m useless", check their mood. Ask about self-harm if needed.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Self-concept: how you see yourself.\n• Self-esteem: how you feel about yourself overall.',

    'presentation::Attribution style': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is how a child explains why things go well or badly.\n• Some think "I did well because I tried a good way". Others think "I did badly because I\'m not smart".\n• Blaming low ability leads to giving up. Blaming the way of working leads to trying again.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• "I\'m just not smart."\n• Saying good results were luck.\n• Avoiding hard work.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Praise effort, good ways of working and improvement. Avoid "you are so smart".\n• After success, name what worked. After a mistake, name what to try next.\n• Do not tell a child who already tries hard to "just try harder".\n• Use words like "not yet" with the whole class.\n• Posters or one-off programmes are not enough on their own.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• Teachers and parents, through daily feedback.\n• If a child blames themselves for everything bad, check for low mood.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Attribution: the reason you give for why something happened.\n• Strategy: a way of doing a task.',

    'presentation::Response to failure and setback': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is how a child reacts when something goes wrong. Examples are a wrong answer, a bad mark, or losing a game.\n• Some bounce back. Others cry, get angry, tear up work or refuse to go on.\n• A big reaction often means a mistake feels like proof they are no good.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Tearing up work.\n• Meltdowns at losing.\n• Refusing to try again after a wrong answer.\n• Being corrected in front of others is a common trigger.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Show that mistakes are normal. Teachers can make and fix mistakes openly.\n• Correct the child quietly, with a next step.\n• A plan for setbacks: a short break, a calming trick, then a smaller task.\n• Practise losing in small, safe games.\n• Do not remove all failure. Children need safe practice at bouncing back.\n• At home, let the child see you fix your own mistakes.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• Primary care or CAMHS, if there is wider worry or low mood.\n• If there is self-harm or talk of being worthless, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Setback: when something goes wrong.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.',

    'presentation::Perfectionism and fear of getting it wrong': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is when a child sets very high standards and is hard on themselves.\n• High standards can be a strength. The problem is fear of mistakes and feeling like a failure.\n• It is not the same as working hard. It is not OCD, though the two can overlap.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Rubbing out again and again, or tearing pages.\n• Not handing in work. Very slow work.\n• Tears at one small mistake.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Talk about the work, not the child\'s worth.\n• Praise finishing: "You got it done."\n• Set limits: "first draft only", or a timer for rewriting.\n• Low-stakes tasks where mistakes are expected.\n• Adults talk about their own mistakes.\n• Do not say "just relax". The child hears that you do not understand.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• Primary care or CAMHS, if there is strong worry, low mood, or rituals.\n• A family doctor quickly, if there are eating or weight worries.\n• If there is self-harm or hopelessness, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• OCD: Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder. Unwanted thoughts, and feeling you must do things over and over.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Rituals: things a person feels they must do in the same way each time.',

    'presentation::Self-esteem as a consequence of unidentified SLD': 'WHAT IT IS\n• A child tried and failed for years. Nobody noticed they had a learning difficulty.\n• So they decided they were "stupid" or "lazy". That is not true.\n• Check which came first, the learning difficulty or the low self-esteem.\n• Many children with dyslexia feel fine about themselves, when they get support.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Saying "I\'m thick".\n• Avoiding reading.\n• Behaviour that hides the difficulty.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Explain the difficulty clearly and kindly: "Your brain learns in a different way. It is not about being clever."\n• Give the right teaching for the learning difficulty. Do not wait.\n• Show progress. Say success comes from effort and good ways of working.\n• Protect time for things the child is good at.\n• Parents may feel guilty or angry it was not spotted. What matters now is that support starts.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The school, with extra teaching.\n• The family doctor or CAMHS, if low mood lasts or there is self-harm.\n• If there is hopelessness or talk of suicide, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SLD: Specific Learning Disorder, like dyslexia. It affects reading, writing or maths.\n• Dyslexia: a lasting difficulty with reading and spelling.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.',

    'presentation::School anxiety presentations (not a standalone diagnosis)': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is worry that shows at school. It may be about work, leaving a parent, teachers, other pupils, or coming to school.\n• The worry is real. But "school anxiety" is not a diagnosis.\n• We describe what the worry is about, and when it happens.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Tummy aches on school mornings.\n• Clinging, or tears at the door.\n• Worry about tests or some teachers.\n• Missing school. This is not the same as skipping school in secret.\nWHAT HELPS\n• A named adult who meets the child each morning. A quiet safe place.\n• Small, planned steps back into what scares them.\n• Staying at home makes the next day harder.\n• Teach ways to cope, like breathing and noticing worry.\n• Parents stay calm, confident and the same each morning.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor, to rule out a physical cause.\n• Primary Care Psychology or CAMHS.\n• If there is self-harm or talk of suicide, act the same day.\n• If the worry may be about abuse or something at home, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Anxiety: strong worry or fear.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

    'presentation::Test and exam anxiety': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is worry about tests and exams that makes a pupil do worse than they know.\n• Worry takes up space in the mind that is needed for thinking.\n• Some nerves are normal and can help. It is a problem when worry harms results or leads to avoiding tests.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Tummy aches on test days.\n• Good class work but poor test results.\n• Missing exams or not handing in work.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Practise under exam-like conditions, step by step. See the exam room first.\n• Write worries down before a test. Use breathing tricks.\n• Do not share results in public. Focus on learning, not grades.\n• A separate room or rest breaks in school exams.\n• For State exams, check the SEC rules. Worry alone may not qualify.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• The family doctor, Primary Care Psychology or CAMHS, if worry is wider or severe.\n• If there are panic attacks, self-harm or talk of suicide, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Anxiety: strong worry or fear.',

    'presentation::Performance anxiety in oral work': 'WHAT IT IS\n• This is worry about speaking in front of others. Examples are reading aloud, answering, talks and oral exams.\n• The pupil worries others are judging them.\n• It is not just shyness. It causes real upset and avoiding.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Freezing, or tummy aches, on days with oral tasks.\n• Speaking very quietly, or not looking at people.\n• Sometimes the cause is a language difficulty or a stammer. The worry may come from that.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Start small. Talk in pairs, then small groups, then bigger ones.\n• Give questions or reading ahead of time. Let them practise with a trusted adult.\n• Allow a recording or smaller audience for school orals.\n• For State exams, check the SEC rules.\n• At home, practise reading aloud, or record it.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The teacher and the school.\n• The family doctor, primary care or CAMHS, if the worry is wider.\n• A speech and language therapist, if language or a stammer is involved.\n• If a child does not speak at all in school but speaks at home, refer to health services.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• SEC: State Examinations Commission. It runs State exams in Ireland.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Stammer: when speech gets stuck, or sounds repeat.',

    'presentation::Anticipatory anxiety about transitions': "WHAT IT IS\n• This is worry that grows before a big change.\n• Examples are starting school, a new class, or moving to secondary school.\n• The worry is about what is coming, not what is happening now.\n• It usually settles once the child is there and knows what it is like.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Asking the same questions about the change again and again.\n• Poor sleep as the date gets closer.\n• Clinging, grumpiness or tummy aches.\n• Things get worse in the term before a move.\nWHAT HELPS\n• Prepare early. Visit the new school. Use photos, a map and the timetable.\n• A named person in the new school who meets the child before and on the first day.\n• A written plan shared with the new school, with parents' agreement.\n• A countdown calendar for some children.\n• Agree a set time each evening to talk about the move. Constant reassurance can keep worry going.\n• Holding a child back a year rarely takes away the worry.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• Both schools, working together.\n• Primary care or CAMHS, if worry is severe or lasts after the move.\n• If there is self-harm or talk of suicide, act the same day.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Transition: moving from one school, class or stage to another.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.",

    'presentation::Somatic complaints presenting at school (tummy aches, headaches)': 'WHAT IT IS\n• These are body pains, like tummy aches and headaches, that keep coming back at school.\n• The pain is real. Worry can cause real pain. The child is not faking.\n• A doctor must check first for a physical cause.\nWHAT YOU MIGHT NOTICE\n• Tummy aches on Monday mornings.\n• Headaches before tests.\n• Many trips to the office, calls home, or missed school.\nWHAT HELPS\n• See the family doctor first.\n• Have the same plan each time. Give kind attention and a short rest in a quiet place. Then go back to class.\n• Going home each time can make the next morning harder.\n• Look at what is hard at the times the pain happens.\n• Explain simply how worry can affect the tummy. Teach breathing and relaxing.\n• Do not tell parents it is "just worry" before the doctor has checked.\nWHO CAN HELP\n• The family doctor. See them urgently if there is weight loss, vomiting, blood, fever, or bad headaches.\n• Primary Care Psychology or CAMHS, if worry or low mood is strong.\n• If there are signs of abuse, or fear of going home, tell Tusla (the child and family agency) quickly. Telling the school\'s safeguarding person is not enough on its own.\nWORDS TO KNOW\n• Somatic: to do with the body.\n• CAMHS: Child and Adolescent Mental Health Services.\n• Tusla: the child and family agency in Ireland.',

}
